  "query_preview": "..\n\nAn cold front is in the process of clearing SE MI and will bring \nabout breezy conditions, steadily falling temperatures, and periodic \nrain showers for today. The departure of low pressure from Michigan \ninto Ontario and Quebec will have cold air advection ramp up through \nthe day as low level flow backs from west to north-northwest. The \nadvection of cooler air will greatly dampen or nullify the typical \ndiurnal peak in temperatures this afternoon as they fall from the \n50s-60s this morning into the 40s by the evening. This will be \nreinforced from a secondary cold front that will progress across SE \nMI late this morning into the early afternoon hours. Passage of this \nsecondary frontal boundary will further increase mixing depths, \nproducing breezy conditions with gusts ranging between 30-35 mph. \nSome isolated potential to 40 mph will be possible after the passage \nof the secondary cold front, but the absence of appreciable stronger\nwind speeds within the mixed layer lowers confidence for higher \nfrequency gusts to 40+ mph. The exception will be within the Thumb\ngiven reduced surface friction along the lake as flow backs to the \nNNW.\n\nRetainment of appreciable low-level moisture wrapped in cyclonic \nflow coupled with shallow instability within the mixed layer will \nsupport drizzle and rain shower chances through the afternoon and \nevening hours. Coverage of rain showers will start to decrease in \nthe evening hours for most locations as flow backs to the north, \nwhich cuts off the additive moisture advection derived from Lake \nMichigan. Consequently, coverage will likely increase around the \nLake Huron shoreline once wind direction orients more favorably off \nof Lake Huron. The enhanced pocket of shallow instability coming off\nthe lake will supplement moisture advection onshore. Notably \nstronger lapse rates extending through 700mb and positive buoyancy \nextending through the -10C isotherm could support more vigorous \nconvective shower development and/or isolated thunderstorms, capable\nof producing small hail. \n\nStrong high pressure will rapidly build in from west to east \novernight which will scour and advect out low-level stratus as dry \nair works in. Clear skies coupled with the early day cold air \nadvection will bring chilly overnight lows. Efficient radiational \ncooling drop temperatures into the low to mid 30s, bringing high \npotential for patchy to widespread frost. The coldest temperatures \nwill be found across the interior of SE MI through the Tri-Cities \nwhere temperatures in the upper 20s to low 30s are possible. Below \nnormal temperatures hold tomorrow afternoon with highs capped in the\nlow to mid 50s. \n\nHigh pressure then sets up and expands over the Midwest and Great \nLakes next week which will bring an extended period of dry weather. \nTemperatures rebound back to above normal values in the mid to upper\n60s, possibly low 70s, by the late week period. This will bring a\nperiod of idyllic fall weather under mostly clear skies for a greater\nportion of next week.",
